\date{2026-10-07}
\title{Flat distributive lattices}
\author{ulrikbuchholtz}
\taxon{Module}
\import{stt-macros}

\p{A distributive lattice is \em{flat} when it is a filtered colimit of free
finitely generated distributive lattices. The flat lattices are the models of
a geometric theory, whose classifying topos is the topos of set-valued functors
on the category of free finitely generated distributive lattices. By Birkhoff
duality that category is opposite to the Dedekind cube category, so this is
the topos of cubical spaces.
This module defines flatness by Lazard's criterion (\ref{ub-004Q}), puts it in
equational form (\ref{ub-004D}), and shows that a flat lattice is nontrivial,
local and colocal (\ref{ub-004R}, \ref{ub-004B}, \ref{ub-004E}). Why these do
not characterise it, and why nothing finite does, is \ref{ub-0041}.}

\agda{
module Algebra.Lattice.Flat where

open import Axioms.UF
}

\agda-imports{
\agda{
open import Foundations.Prelude
open import Foundations.Fin

open import Modalities.Instances.Truncation

open import Core.Joins
open import Core.SetQuotients

open import Algebra.Lattice
open import Algebra.Lattice.Instances.Bool
open import Algebra.Lattice.Instances.Free.Multivariate
  Bool-Lattice Bool-distributive
open import Algebra.Lattice.Presentations Bool-Lattice Bool-distributive
}
}

\subtree[ub-004Q]{
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Flat distributive lattices}

\p{An algebra over a Lawvere theory is a filtered colimit of free finitely
generated algebras exactly when every homomorphism into it from a finitely
presented algebra factors through a free finitely generated one. This is
Lazard's criterion, proved for modules in \citet{Thm. 1.2}{Laz69}. For an
arbitrary algebraic theory, indeed for any clan, a model is a filtered
colimit of free finitely generated ones exactly when its category of elements
over those is filtered \citet{Lem. 5.4}{Fre26}, and the factorisation
property, applied also to coequalisers, is what makes it filtered. It is the
only characterisation of flatness available in general. Distributive lattices
are the algebras over the [initial one](stt-00S8), #{\mathrm{Bool}}, so
presentations and free lattices are those of
[#{\mathrm{Bool}}-algebras](ub-001M), and every lattice is a
#{\mathrm{Bool}}-algebra in a unique way.}

\agda{
Bool-Algebra : ∀ {𝓤} {B : Type 𝓤} (M : Lattice B) → Algebra Bool-Lattice B
Bool-Algebra M .Algebra.str-Lattice = M
Bool-Algebra M .Algebra.ι = Bool-initial M .centre .fst
Bool-Algebra M .Algebra.ι-alg = Bool-initial M .centre .snd
}

\p{For a distributive lattice #{M} the criterion is the scheme of geometric
sequents, one for each finite presentation
#{\langle x_1, \ldots, x_k \mid R \rangle},}

##{R(\vec x) \;\vdash_{\vec x}\; \bigvee_{n \in \mathbb{N}}\;
  \bigvee_{\substack{\vec q \in F(n)^k \\ R(\vec q)}}\;
  \exists \vec y.\; \vec x = \vec q(\vec y),}

\p{where #{F(n)} is the free distributive lattice on #{n} generators: a tuple
in #{M} satisfying the relations is the image of a tuple of polynomials
satisfying them, evaluated at some point of #{M^n}. The inner disjunction is
finite, since #{F(n)} is, and the outer one is not. As a type, flatness says
that every homomorphism #{x : A \to M} out of a presented lattice merely
factors as a homomorphism #{h : A \to F(n)} followed by
[evaluation](ub-004M) at a point #{y} of #{M^n}.}

\quiver{
\begin{tikzcd}
	A && M \\
	\\
	& {F(n)}
	\arrow["x", from=1-1, to=1-3]
	\arrow["h"', dashed, from=1-1, to=3-2]
	\arrow["{\mathrm{ev}_y}"', from=3-2, to=1-3]
\end{tikzcd}
}

\agda{
module _ {𝓤} {B : Type 𝓤} (M : Lattice B) (d : is-distributive M) where

  factors-through-free
    : (p : Presentation) → LatticeAlgHom (Presented p) (Bool-Algebra M)
    → Type 𝓤
  factors-through-free p x
    = Σ[ n ∶ ℕ ] Σ[ h ∶ LatticeAlgHom (Presented p) (Polynomial-Algebra n) ]
      Σ[ y ∶ (Fin n → B) ]
        (x ＝ LatticeAlgHom-∘ (Polynomial-eval (Bool-Algebra M) d n y) h)

  is-flat : Type 𝓤
  is-flat
    = (p : Presentation) (x : LatticeAlgHom (Presented p) (Bool-Algebra M))
    → ║ factors-through-free p x ║₋₁

  is-flat-is-prop : is-prop is-flat
  is-flat-is-prop = Π-is-prop λ _ → Π-is-prop λ _ → ║-║₋₁-is-prop
}
}

\subtree[ub-004D]{
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Flatness in equational form}
\date{2026-10-06}

\p{Unfolded, flatness says that a solution #{v} in #{M} of a finite system of
equations is a specialisation of a solution in polynomials. There is an #{n},
a map of algebras #{g : F(k) \to F(n)} that respects the equations, and a
point #{y} of #{M^n} with #{v_i = g(x_i)(y)}. A solution is a homomorphism out
of the presented lattice, by its [recursion principle](ub-001M), and a map
out of the presented lattice restricts to a map out of the free one that
respects the relations.}

\agda{
module _ {𝓤} {B : Type 𝓤} (M : Lattice B) (d : is-distributive M) where

  solution-in-polynomials
    : (p : Presentation) → (Fin (Presentation.generators p) → B) → Type 𝓤
  solution-in-polynomials p v
    = Σ[ n ∶ ℕ ]
      Σ[ g ∶ LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra (Presentation.generators p))
                           (Polynomial-Algebra n) ]
      ( respects p g
      × (Σ[ y ∶ (Fin n → B) ] (v ~ Polynomial-specialise (Bool-Algebra M) d n y g)))

  solutions-factor
    : is-flat M d
    → (p : Presentation) (v : Fin (Presentation.generators p) → B)
    → respects p (Polynomial-eval (Bool-Algebra M) d _ v)
    → ║ solution-in-polynomials p v ║₋₁
  solutions-factor flat p v r
    = Trunc.ind (λ _ → trunc!)
        (λ where
          (n , h , y , e) →
            Trunc.η
              ( n
              , LatticeAlgHom-∘ h (Presented-ι p)
              , Presented-ι-respects p h
              , y
              , λ k → sym (Polynomial-eval-gen (Bool-Algebra M) d _ v k)
                    ∙ sym (Presented-rec-β p ev-v r (Polynomial-gen k))
                    ∙ happly (ap (λ f → f .fst .fst) e)
                        (ι-quot (Polynomial-gen k))))
        (flat p (Presented-rec p ev-v r))
    where
      ev-v = Polynomial-eval (Bool-Algebra M) d _ v
}
}

\subtree[ub-004R]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Flat lattices are nontrivial}
\date{2026-10-07}

\p{In a flat distributive lattice #{0 \neq 1}. If #{0 = 1}, then the empty
tuple solves the equation #{0 = 1} with no unknowns, so by \ref{ub-004D}
there is a map of algebras #{g : F(0) \to F(n)} with #{g(0) = g(1)}.
Evaluating at any vertex of the cube in #{\mathrm{Bool}} turns this into
#{\mathrm{false} = \mathrm{true}}.}

\agda{
0=1-presentation : Presentation
0=1-presentation = mk-presentation 0 1 (λ _ → false) (λ _ → true)

module _ {𝓤} {B : Type 𝓤} (M : Lattice B) (d : is-distributive M) where
  open Lattice M using (0l ; 1l)

  flat-is-nontrivial : is-flat M d → 0l ≠ 1l
  flat-is-nontrivial flat e
    = Trunc.ind (λ _ → is-truncated←is-prop λ ())
        (λ (n , g , r , _) → from-free n g r)
        (solutions-factor M d flat 0=1-presentation (λ ()) respects-0=1)
    where
      respects-0=1
        : respects 0=1-presentation (Polynomial-eval (Bool-Algebra M) d 0 (λ ()))
      respects-0=1 _
        = hom .is-lattice-hom.pres-0 ∙ e ∙ sym (hom .is-lattice-hom.pres-1)
        where hom = Polynomial-eval-is-lattice-hom (Bool-Algebra M) d 0 (λ ())

      from-free
        : (n : ℕ) (g : LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra 0) (Polynomial-Algebra n))
        → respects 0=1-presentation g → ∅
      from-free n g r
        = true≠false
            (sym (hom .is-lattice-hom.pres-1)
            ∙ ap (Polynomial-eval-at n (λ _ → false) .fst .fst) (sym (r fzero))
            ∙ hom .is-lattice-hom.pres-0)
        where
          hom
            = un-is-lattice-alg-hom
                (LatticeAlgHom-∘ (Polynomial-eval-at n (λ _ → false)) g .snd)
}
}

\subtree[ub-004B]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Flat lattices are local}
\date{2026-10-06}

\p{In a flat distributive lattice, #{i \lor j = 1} implies #{i = 1} or
#{j = 1}. The pair #{(i, j)} solves #{x_0 \lor x_1 = 1}, so by \ref{ub-004D}
it is merely #{(q_0(y), q_1(y))} for polynomials #{q_0 \lor q_1 = 1} in #{n}
variables and some point #{y}. Evaluating at the bottom vertex of the cube
in #{\mathrm{Bool}} gives #{a = q_0(0)} and #{b = q_1(0)} with
#{a \lor b = 1} in #{\mathrm{Bool}}, where one of the two is #{1}. Say
#{a = 1}. Evaluation commutes with the map #{\mathrm{Bool} \to M}
(\ref{ub-004M}), so #{q_0(0) = 1} in #{M} too, and then
#{q_0(y) = 1} because polynomials are [monotone](ub-004A).}

\p{The equation is presented by two generators and one relation, and
#{(i, j)} is a solution of it in #{M}. A solution in polynomials specialises
to a solution at every point of any lattice, in particular at the vertices of
the cube in #{\mathrm{Bool}}.}

\agda{
open Lattice (Polynomial-Lattice 2) using ()
  renaming (_∨_ to _∨ₓ_ ; _∧_ to _∧ₓ_ ; 0l to 0ₓ ; 1l to 1ₓ)

∨=1-presentation : Presentation
∨=1-presentation
  = mk-presentation 2 1
      (λ _ → Polynomial-gen fzero ∨ₓ Polynomial-gen (fsuc fzero)) (λ _ → 1ₓ)

pair : ∀ {𝓤} {B : Type 𝓤} → B → B → Fin 2 → B
pair i j fzero = i
pair i j (fsuc fzero) = j

module _
  {𝓦} {C : Type 𝓦} (N : Algebra Bool-Lattice C)
  (Nd : is-distributive-algebra N)
  where
  open Algebra N using (_∨_ ; _∧_ ; 0l ; 1l)

  pair-respects-∨=1
    : (i j : C) → i ∨ j ＝ 1l
    → respects ∨=1-presentation (Polynomial-eval N Nd 2 (pair i j))
  pair-respects-∨=1 i j e _
    = hom .is-lattice-hom.pres-∨
        {Polynomial-gen fzero} {Polynomial-gen (fsuc fzero)}
    ∙ ap₂ _∨_ (Polynomial-eval-gen N Nd 2 (pair i j) fzero)
        (Polynomial-eval-gen N Nd 2 (pair i j) (fsuc fzero))
    ∙ e
    ∙ sym (hom .is-lattice-hom.pres-1)
    where hom = Polynomial-eval-is-lattice-hom N Nd 2 (pair i j)

  specialise-∨=1
    : (n : ℕ) (y : Fin n → C)
      (g : LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra 2) (Polynomial-Algebra n))
    → respects ∨=1-presentation g
    → Polynomial-specialise N Nd n y g fzero
    ∨ Polynomial-specialise N Nd n y g (fsuc fzero) ＝ 1l
  specialise-∨=1 n y g r
    = sym (hom .is-lattice-hom.pres-∨
            {Polynomial-gen fzero} {Polynomial-gen (fsuc fzero)})
    ∙ ap (Polynomial-eval N Nd n y .fst .fst) (r fzero)
    ∙ hom .is-lattice-hom.pres-1
    where
      hom
        = un-is-lattice-alg-hom
            (LatticeAlgHom-∘ (Polynomial-eval N Nd n y) g .snd)

||-＝true : (a b : Bool) → (a || b) ＝ true → (a ＝ true) ⊎ (b ＝ true)
||-＝true true b e = inl refl
||-＝true false true e = inr refl
||-＝true false false ()

module _ {𝓤} {B : Type 𝓤} (M : Lattice B) (d : is-distributive M) where
  open Lattice M

  either-＝1-is-prop : {i j : B} → is-prop ((i ＝ 1l) * (j ＝ 1l))
  either-＝1-is-prop = *-is-prop (carrier-is-set _ _) (carrier-is-set _ _)

  flat-is-local
    : is-flat M d → (i j : B) → i ∨ j ＝ 1l → (i ＝ 1l) * (j ＝ 1l)
  flat-is-local flat i j e
    = Trunc.ind (λ _ → is-truncated←is-prop either-＝1-is-prop)
        (λ (n , g , r , y , s) → from-free n g r y s)
        (solutions-factor M d flat ∨=1-presentation (pair i j)
          (pair-respects-∨=1 (Bool-Algebra M) d i j e))
    where
      from-free
        : (n : ℕ)
          (g : LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra 2) (Polynomial-Algebra n))
          (r : respects ∨=1-presentation g) (y : Fin n → B)
        → pair i j ~ Polynomial-specialise (Bool-Algebra M) d n y g
        → (i ＝ 1l) * (j ＝ 1l)
      from-free n g r y s
        = case (||-＝true _ _
                 (specialise-∨=1 (initial-Algebra Bool-Lattice)
                   Bool-distributive n (λ _ → false) g r))
        where
          vertex : (k : Fin 2) → Bool
          vertex = Polynomial-specialise (initial-Algebra Bool-Lattice)
                     Bool-distributive n (λ _ → false) g

          lift : (k : Fin 2) → vertex k ＝ true → pair i j k ＝ 1l
          lift k a
            = s k
            ∙ ＝1←1≤
                (≤-trans
                  (≤←＝
                    ( sym (ap (Algebra.ι (Bool-Algebra M)) a)
                    ∙ sym (Polynomial-eval-ι (Bool-Algebra M) d n
                            (λ _ → false) (g .fst .fst (Polynomial-gen k)))))
                  (Polynomial-eval-is-monotone (Bool-Algebra M) d n
                    (λ _ → 0l) y (λ _ → 0-init)
                    (g .fst .fst (Polynomial-gen k))))

          case
            : (vertex fzero ＝ true) ⊎ (vertex (fsuc fzero) ＝ true)
            → (i ＝ 1l) * (j ＝ 1l)
          case (inl a) = ι₁ (lift fzero a)
          case (inr b) = ι₂ (lift (fsuc fzero) b)
}
}

\subtree[ub-004E]{
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Flat lattices are colocal}
\date{2026-10-06}

\p{Dually, in a flat distributive lattice #{i \land j = 0} implies #{i = 0}
or #{j = 0}: the argument of \ref{ub-004B} with the top vertex in place of
the bottom one.}

\agda{
∧=0-presentation : Presentation
∧=0-presentation
  = mk-presentation 2 1
      (λ _ → Polynomial-gen fzero ∧ₓ Polynomial-gen (fsuc fzero)) (λ _ → 0ₓ)

module _
  {𝓦} {C : Type 𝓦} (N : Algebra Bool-Lattice C)
  (Nd : is-distributive-algebra N)
  where
  open Algebra N using (_∨_ ; _∧_ ; 0l ; 1l)

  pair-respects-∧=0
    : (i j : C) → i ∧ j ＝ 0l
    → respects ∧=0-presentation (Polynomial-eval N Nd 2 (pair i j))
  pair-respects-∧=0 i j e _
    = hom .is-lattice-hom.pres-∧
        {Polynomial-gen fzero} {Polynomial-gen (fsuc fzero)}
    ∙ ap₂ _∧_ (Polynomial-eval-gen N Nd 2 (pair i j) fzero)
        (Polynomial-eval-gen N Nd 2 (pair i j) (fsuc fzero))
    ∙ e
    ∙ sym (hom .is-lattice-hom.pres-0)
    where hom = Polynomial-eval-is-lattice-hom N Nd 2 (pair i j)

  specialise-∧=0
    : (n : ℕ) (y : Fin n → C)
      (g : LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra 2) (Polynomial-Algebra n))
    → respects ∧=0-presentation g
    → Polynomial-specialise N Nd n y g fzero
    ∧ Polynomial-specialise N Nd n y g (fsuc fzero) ＝ 0l
  specialise-∧=0 n y g r
    = sym (hom .is-lattice-hom.pres-∧
            {Polynomial-gen fzero} {Polynomial-gen (fsuc fzero)})
    ∙ ap (Polynomial-eval N Nd n y .fst .fst) (r fzero)
    ∙ hom .is-lattice-hom.pres-0
    where
      hom
        = un-is-lattice-alg-hom
            (LatticeAlgHom-∘ (Polynomial-eval N Nd n y) g .snd)

&-＝false : (a b : Bool) → (a & b) ＝ false → (a ＝ false) ⊎ (b ＝ false)
&-＝false false b e = inl refl
&-＝false true false e = inr refl
&-＝false true true ()

module _ {𝓤} {B : Type 𝓤} (M : Lattice B) (d : is-distributive M) where
  open Lattice M

  either-＝0-is-prop : {i j : B} → is-prop ((i ＝ 0l) * (j ＝ 0l))
  either-＝0-is-prop = *-is-prop (carrier-is-set _ _) (carrier-is-set _ _)

  flat-is-colocal
    : is-flat M d → (i j : B) → i ∧ j ＝ 0l → (i ＝ 0l) * (j ＝ 0l)
  flat-is-colocal flat i j e
    = Trunc.ind (λ _ → is-truncated←is-prop either-＝0-is-prop)
        (λ (n , g , r , y , s) → from-free n g r y s)
        (solutions-factor M d flat ∧=0-presentation (pair i j)
          (pair-respects-∧=0 (Bool-Algebra M) d i j e))
    where
      from-free
        : (n : ℕ)
          (g : LatticeAlgHom (Polynomial-Algebra 2) (Polynomial-Algebra n))
          (r : respects ∧=0-presentation g) (y : Fin n → B)
        → pair i j ~ Polynomial-specialise (Bool-Algebra M) d n y g
        → (i ＝ 0l) * (j ＝ 0l)
      from-free n g r y s
        = case (&-＝false _ _
                 (specialise-∧=0 (initial-Algebra Bool-Lattice)
                   Bool-distributive n (λ _ → true) g r))
        where
          vertex : (k : Fin 2) → Bool
          vertex = Polynomial-specialise (initial-Algebra Bool-Lattice)
                     Bool-distributive n (λ _ → true) g

          lift : (k : Fin 2) → vertex k ＝ false → pair i j k ＝ 0l
          lift k a
            = s k
            ∙ ＝0←≤0
                (≤-trans
                  (Polynomial-eval-is-monotone (Bool-Algebra M) d n
                    y (λ _ → 1l) (λ _ → 1-top)
                    (g .fst .fst (Polynomial-gen k)))
                  (≤←＝
                    ( Polynomial-eval-ι (Bool-Algebra M) d n
                        (λ _ → true) (g .fst .fst (Polynomial-gen k))
                    ∙ ap (Algebra.ι (Bool-Algebra M)) a)))

          case
            : (vertex fzero ＝ false) ⊎ (vertex (fsuc fzero) ＝ false)
            → (i ＝ 0l) * (j ＝ 0l)
          case (inl a) = ι₁ (lift fzero a)
          case (inr b) = ι₂ (lift (fsuc fzero) b)
}
}

\subtree[ub-0041]{
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Why flatness is not a finite matter}

\p{Three coherent sequents that every flat lattice satisfies are
\em{nontriviality}, #{0 \neq 1}, and \em{locality} in both forms,
#{x \lor y = 1 \Rightarrow x = 1 \lor y = 1} and
#{x \land y = 0 \Rightarrow x = 0 \lor y = 0}: in a free lattice, evaluation
at the bottom vertex of the cube is a lattice map to #{\mathrm{Bool}} whose
kernel is #{\{1\}}, and evaluation at the top vertex one whose kernel is
#{\{0\}}, and all three sequents are preserved by filtered colimits. Their
orthogonality forms are the [disjunctive](stt-00DY) and
[conjunctive](stt-00TW) conditions, and \citek{SY25} call them (NT), (L) and
(cL). They do not characterise flatness, and no finite list of coherent
sequents does.}

\p{Flatness implies all three, for any distributive lattice
(\ref{ub-004R}, \ref{ub-004B}, \ref{ub-004E}), by evaluating at the vertices
of a cube in #{\mathrm{Bool}}. In triangulated type theory the interval is
assumed to be nontrivial, and for a flat interval that assumption is a
consequence.}

\p{\strong{They do not suffice.} Finitely presented distributive lattices are
finite, and a finite lattice #{A} is flat exactly when it is a retract of a
free one; under Birkhoff duality that says that the poset #{J(A)} of
join-irreducibles is a poset retract of a cube, which is to say a lattice.
Nontriviality and the two localities, on the other hand, say only that
#{J(A)} is nonempty with a top and a bottom. So the downset lattice of the
bounded poset #{\{0 < a, b < c, d < 1\}} with #{a, b} both below #{c, d} is
nontrivial, local and colocal, and not flat. That poset is the smallest
bounded poset that is not a lattice.}

\quiver{
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=small, row sep=small]
	& 1 \\
	c && d \\
	a && b \\
	& 0
	\arrow[no head, from=4-2, to=3-1]
	\arrow[no head, from=4-2, to=3-3]
	\arrow[no head, from=3-1, to=2-1]
	\arrow[no head, from=3-1, to=2-3]
	\arrow[no head, from=3-3, to=2-1]
	\arrow[no head, from=3-3, to=2-3]
	\arrow[no head, from=2-1, to=1-2]
	\arrow[no head, from=2-3, to=1-2]
\end{tikzcd}
}

\p{The elements #{a} and #{b} have two minimal upper bounds, #{c} and #{d},
and so no join.}

\p{\strong{No finite list does.} The models of a coherent theory are closed
under ultraproducts, and the flat lattices are not, because the dimension #{n}
in the scheme of \ref{ub-004Q} cannot be bounded in terms of the presentation.
Take #{A = D(P)} for the poset #{P} just named, so that a homomorphism #{A \to
F(m) = D([1]^m)} is a monotone map #{g : [1]^m \to P}, and let #{g} send the
vertex #{e_i} to #{a} for odd #{i} and to #{b} for even #{i}, a sum #{e_i +
e_j} to #{c} or #{d} according to the parity of #{i + j}, and every vertex of
weight at least three to #{1}. A factorisation of #{g} through a free lattice
#{F(n)} is a factorisation of the poset map through a lattice #{L} that is a
retract of #{[1]^n}, and in #{L} the elements #{x_i \lor x_{i+1}} for #{i} odd
are pairwise distinct: were two of them equal to a common #{u}, some cross
join #{x_i \lor x_k} of the other parity would lie below #{u}, putting #{u} in
both #{r^{-1}(\uparrow c)} and #{r^{-1}(\uparrow d)} and hence in
#{r^{-1}(1)}. So #{|L| \ge m/2} and #{n \ge \log_2(m/2)}. The maps #{g_m}
therefore assemble to a homomorphism from #{A} into a nonprincipal
ultraproduct of the #{F(m)} which factors through no #{F(n)} at all.}
}
